  "method": "Complete native modules; pre-generated inputs checked against Integer gcd outside timing; direct unboxed calls; alternating order; changing corpus and nonce per sample; calibrated call count; CPU and wall clocks; paired medians and 10000 bootstrap resamples.",
  "scope": "64-bit native backend, host GHC 9.12 code generation. Multiword cases compose native remainder and word GCD on valid multiword operands. No full patched compiler, public Integer instance, or GMP-backend speedup is measured. Timed allocation statistics include fixed measurement bookkeeping.",
  "validation": {
    "all_paired_checksums_match": true,
    "input_pair_checks_per_version": 1638400
  },
  "findings": [
    "Random 32-bit and 64-bit word inputs improve about 1.25x; shared powers of two improve about 1.31x.",
    "Native multiword-remainder plus word-GCD composition improves about 1.22x at 128 bits and 1.15x at 256 bits. The 1024- and 4096-bit confidence intervals include 1.0, so these measurements do not establish a gain there.",
    "Neighboring inputs regress from about 2.52 to 3.05 ns; zero second operands regress from 1.54 to 1.66 ns. Equal, exactly divisible and unit cases do not show a clear change.",
    "CPU and wall-time results agree. No sample had a timed garbage collection; allocation counters include only small fixed timing/bookkeeping costs.",
    "These compare the native backend before/after. The installed compiler and its public Integer/Natural instances are unchanged; there is no demonstrated GMP-backed application gain.",
    "Bootstrap intervals describe these 15 paired samples on one machine and compiler, not portability or whole-application performance."
  ],
